#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Guided setup for Saber: check the machine, pick a model, toggle the common options, then install
the missing pieces and launch the server.

    tools/saber-setup                 # the terminal UI
    tools/saber-setup --print         # no UI: print the plan for the saved (or default) settings
    tools/saber-setup --model flashnext --print
    tools/saber-setup --tp 1 --print  # the 27B on ONE card (TP=1)

Python 3.11+ standard library only (curses, tomllib). The UI never decides anything the launchers
do not: every option maps to an environment variable or a flag that tools/serve_27b.sh /
tools/serve_flashnext.sh already take (README "Flags and options").

Settings live in the SAME file the browser console (`saber ui`) uses, ~/.config/saber/config.toml,
so the terminal wizard and the browser agree: this tool reads its options from the console's
engine section and writes back only the keys it owns (preset, port, its flags, the NVMe path and
the [setup] state). An older ~/.config/saber/setup.json is migrated into config.toml on the first
run that saves. The model list is tools/models.json (the console embeds the same file). A
re-runnable ~/.config/saber/launch-<model>.sh is still written on request.
"""
